      "title": "9.8.3 Salts of weak acids and strong bases",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 205,
          "pdf_to": 205,
          "note": "Topic 9.8.3, printed page 205."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The example of salt of weak acid and strong base is sodium acetate."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CH3COONa_{(s)} + H2O_{(l)} <=> CH3COO^-_{(aq)} + Na^+_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃COONa(s) + H₂O(l) ⇌ CH₃COO⁻(aq) + Na⁺(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "CH₃COO⁻ is the conjugate base of CH₃COOH (a weak acid) and reacts with water:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CH3COO^-_{(aq)} + H2O_{(l)} <=> CH3COOH_{(aq)} + OH^-_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃COO⁻(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ CH₃COOH(aq) + OH⁻(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Na⁺ is the conjugate acid of NaOH (a strong base) and does not affect the pH. The solution is basic due to the CH₃COO⁻ ion."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
